\fs32\b\cf1 Retail Book Shop\par
\fs28 AKS Technical Implementation Plan\par
\fs20\cf0 Version 1.0 | Scope: backend services, containers, NGINX, Azure load balancing, Redis, Kafka messaging, AKS scalability, CI/CD\par
\par
\b Purpose\b0\par
This document is a step-by-step technical plan for evolving the current React/Vite storefront into a production-ready, scalable platform on Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS). It includes representative configuration code. The code is reference material only and does not modify the current project.\par
\par
\b Current baseline\b0\par
The current repository contains a React/Vite frontend. Catalog data and cart state are currently held in the browser. There are no backend services, Dockerfiles, Kubernetes manifests, Redis, Kafka, or CI/CD deployment files yet. The implementation must therefore proceed from a new backend foundation.\par
\par
\b Target architecture\b0\par
\f1 Internet -> Azure Load Balancer -> NGINX Ingress -> Frontend Service -> Frontend Pods\par
                         NGINX Ingress -> Backend Services -> Backend Pods\par
                                                     -> Managed database\par
                                                     -> Azure Cache for Redis\par
                                                     -> Kafka-compatible messaging\par
                                                     -> Blob Storage, search, payment and email providers\f0\par
\par
\b Recommended deployment principle\b0\par
Treat the fourteen items below as logical service boundaries. Do not automatically create fourteen independently operated services on the first release. Start with a modular backend plus workers, then split a module when independent scaling, ownership, or failure isolation provides a measurable benefit.\par
\page
\fs26\b 1. Delivery phases\b0\fs20\par
\b Phase 0 - decisions and prerequisites\b0\par
1. Confirm traffic, users, regions, compliance, RTO, RPO, availability targets, budget, and corporate network restrictions.\par
2. Select the approved Azure subscription, resource groups, region pair, naming standard, tagging, and identity model.\par
3. Decide whether the message platform must be Apache Kafka or whether Azure Event Hubs Kafka compatibility is acceptable.\par
4. Decide whether NGINX is mandatory or whether Azure Application Gateway for Containers is the enterprise standard.\par
5. Create a threat model and classify customer, payment, operational, and public data.\par
\par
\b Phase 1 - engineering foundation\b0\par
6. Select the backend language and framework supported by the company. Standardize one runtime before building services.\par
7. Create service templates with configuration, logging, request IDs, metrics, tracing, validation, timeouts, graceful shutdown, and health endpoints.\par
8. Define REST/OpenAPI contracts and event schemas before implementation. Version both contracts.\par
9. Create local Compose dependencies for a database, Redis, Kafka-compatible broker, NGINX, search, and observability.\par
10. Create image standards: multi-stage build, non-root user, pinned base image, vulnerability scan, immutable tag, and SBOM.\par
\par
\b Phase 2 - first vertical slice\b0\par
11. Implement catalog, inventory, cart, order, authentication, and frontend integration.\par
12. Add NGINX routing and AKS deployment.\par
13. Add Redis for cache and short-lived state; keep durable records in the database.\par
14. Add Kafka events, outbox processing, retries, and dead-letter handling.\par
15. Load test the checkout path before adding all remaining services.\par
\par
\b Phase 3 - remaining capabilities\b0\par
16. Add wishlist, payment, shipping, notifications, reviews, admin, reporting, and search improvements.\par
17. Split service deployables only after measuring load, release coupling, or team ownership.\par
18. Add production resilience, security, backup restore, observability, and release controls.\par
\page
\fs26\b 2. Service-by-service implementation\b0\fs20\par
\b 2.1 Authentication service\b0\par
Responsibilities: sign-in, sign-up if required, OAuth/OIDC integration, token validation, roles, refresh policy, account lockout, and audit events.\par
Steps:\par
1. Integrate with the corporate identity provider or an approved OIDC provider.\par
2. Keep provider client secrets in Key Vault; never use a secret in a VITE_* browser variable.\par
3. Validate issuer, audience, signature, expiry, nonce, and scopes.\par
4. Issue only short-lived application tokens where necessary.\par
5. Publish UserRegistered, UserRoleChanged, and UserDisabled events.\par
6. Add tests for invalid tokens, replay, missing roles, and disabled accounts.\par
Interface examples: POST /auth/login, POST /auth/refresh, POST /auth/logout, GET /auth/me.\par
Container profile: stateless API; minimum two replicas in production.\par
\par
\b 2.2 User and profile service\b0\par
Responsibilities: customer profile, addresses, preferences, consent, and account status.\par
Steps:\par
1. Create the user schema owned only by this service.\par
2. Store address history and consent changes with audit timestamps.\par
3. Require an authenticated subject ID for all customer operations.\par
4. Publish ProfileUpdated and AddressChanged events.\par
5. Protect personal data with encryption, access control, retention, and deletion procedures.\par
Interface examples: GET /users/me, PATCH /users/me, GET /users/me/addresses.\par
Container profile: stateless API; database is external and durable.\par
\par
\b 2.3 Catalog service\b0\par
Responsibilities: books, authors, categories, descriptions, prices, cover-image references, and publication status.\par
Steps:\par
1. Define a catalog database owned by the catalog service.\par
2. Add admin-only write APIs and public read APIs.\par
3. Validate price currency, ISBN, category, and image metadata.\par
4. Publish BookCreated, BookUpdated, PriceChanged, and BookRemoved events.\par
5. Use Blob Storage for images; store only object references in the database.\par
Interface examples: GET /books, GET /books/{id}, POST /admin/books, PATCH /admin/books/{id}.\par
Container profile: stateless API; horizontally scalable reads.\par
\par
\b 2.4 Search service\b0\par
Responsibilities: free-text search, filtering, sorting, and search indexing.\par
Steps:\par
1. Select Azure AI Search or the approved search engine.\par
2. Consume catalog events using a dedicated consumer group.\par
3. Build an index model separate from the catalog write model.\par
4. Make indexing idempotent and replayable.\par
5. Return a clear stale-index behavior if the search platform is unavailable.\par
6. Monitor indexing lag and failed documents.\par
Interface examples: GET /search?q=history&category=science.\par
Container profile: stateless query API plus event-driven index worker.\par
\par
\b 2.5 Inventory service\b0\par
Responsibilities: stock, reservations, release, adjustment, and availability.\par
Steps:\par
1. Make inventory the only writer of stock quantities.\par
2. Implement atomic reservation with an expiration time.\par
3. Require an idempotency key for reserve and release commands.\par
4. Publish StockReserved, StockReleased, and StockAdjusted events.\par
5. Reject overselling under concurrent checkout load.\par
6. Add reconciliation against warehouse data if applicable.\par
Interface examples: GET /inventory/{bookId}, POST /inventory/reservations, DELETE /inventory/reservations/{id}.\par
Container profile: stateless API; transactional database required.\par
\par
\b 2.6 Cart service\b0\par
Responsibilities: cart lines, quantities, pricing snapshot, expiry, and checkout preparation.\par
Steps:\par
1. Use the authenticated user ID or an anonymous cart ID.\par
2. Store hot cart state in Redis with a defined TTL.\par
3. Persist carts or checkout snapshots durably when business policy requires recovery.\par
4. Reprice and revalidate stock during checkout; never trust browser totals.\par
5. Add idempotent merge behavior when an anonymous user signs in.\par
Interface examples: GET /cart, POST /cart/items, PATCH /cart/items/{bookId}, DELETE /cart/items/{bookId}.\par
Container profile: stateless API; Redis is an optimization, not the order system of record.\par
\par
\b 2.7 Wishlist service\b0\par
Responsibilities: durable saved books and list management.\par
Steps:\par
1. Store wishlist ownership in a durable database.\par
2. Validate that referenced books exist or mark removed products clearly.\par
3. Add authorization tests for cross-user access.\par
4. Publish WishlistItemAdded and WishlistItemRemoved events if reporting needs them.\par
Container profile: stateless API with durable database.\par
\page
\fs26\b 2.8 Order service\b0\fs20\par
Responsibilities: checkout orchestration, order state, totals, history, and status transitions.\par
Steps:\par
1. Define an explicit state machine: Pending, Reserved, PaymentPending, Paid, FulfillmentPending, Shipped, Completed, Cancelled, and Failed.\par
2. Store an immutable price snapshot and delivery snapshot on the order.\par
3. Require an idempotency key for order creation.\par
4. Coordinate inventory reservation and payment through commands and events.\par
5. Publish OrderCreated, OrderPaid, OrderCancelled, and OrderShipped events.\par
6. Implement compensation when payment or inventory fails.\par
Interface examples: POST /orders, GET /orders/{id}, GET /users/me/orders.\par
Container profile: stateless API; durable transactional database required.\par
\par
\b 2.9 Payment integration service\b0\par
Responsibilities: payment-provider adapter, payment intent, status, refunds, and webhook verification.\par
Steps:\par
1. Select an approved payment provider and sandbox.\par
2. Use provider-hosted fields or tokenization; do not store raw card numbers or security codes.\par
3. Store only provider IDs, status, amount, currency, and audit metadata.\par
4. Verify webhook signatures and reject duplicate events.\par
5. Make payment commands and callbacks idempotent.\par
6. Publish PaymentAuthorized, PaymentFailed, and RefundCompleted events.\par
7. Use a private outbound route and monitor provider errors and latency.\par
Container profile: stateless API and webhook worker; secrets in Key Vault.\par
\par
\b 2.10 Shipping service\b0\par
Responsibilities: shipping options, address validation, labels, tracking, and carrier integration.\par
Steps:\par
1. Validate delivery address before payment capture if business policy requires it.\par
2. Integrate with a carrier sandbox first.\par
3. Consume OrderPaid and publish ShipmentCreated, ShipmentDispatched, and DeliveryUpdated.\par
4. Store carrier IDs and tracking status, not carrier credentials in code.\par
5. Make carrier callbacks idempotent.\par
Container profile: stateless API and worker; durable shipment data.\par
\par
\b 2.11 Notification service\b0\par
Responsibilities: email/SMS/push templates and delivery.\par
Steps:\par
1. Consume order, payment, and shipping events.\par
2. Use a provider adapter and store delivery status.\par
3. Apply retry limits and dead-letter failed messages.\par
4. Avoid sending duplicate confirmations by using an event ID as an idempotency key.\par
5. Keep templates versioned and reviewed.\par
Container profile: event-driven workers; scale from Kafka lag.\par
\par
\b 2.12 Review service\b0\par
Responsibilities: ratings, reviews, verification, moderation, and abuse controls.\par
Steps:\par
1. Permit reviews only for eligible customers if required.\par
2. Store moderation state and audit actions.\par
3. Add rate limiting, profanity/abuse screening, and report handling.\par
4. Publish ReviewPublished and ReviewModerated events.\par
Container profile: stateless API; durable database.\par
\par
\b 2.13 Admin service\b0\par
Responsibilities: protected operations for catalog, prices, stock, users, and orders.\par
Steps:\par
1. Use separate admin roles and step-up authentication for sensitive operations.\par
2. Call domain APIs instead of writing other services' tables.\par
3. Log every administrative action with actor, reason, and before/after references.\par
4. Add approval workflow for price and inventory changes where required.\par
Container profile: stateless API; private route or strict authorization.\par
\par
\b 2.14 Reporting service\b0\par
Responsibilities: sales, popular books, revenue, inventory, and operational reports.\par
Steps:\par
1. Consume business events into reporting read models.\par
2. Keep reporting queries away from transactional databases.\par
3. Define event correction and replay procedures.\par
4. Mask or aggregate personal data in reports.\par
5. Add scheduled exports only through controlled jobs.\par
Container profile: API plus event consumer; scale independently from checkout.\par
\page
\fs26\b 3. Container and image implementation\b0\fs20\par
1. Give each deployable a Dockerfile and a health endpoint.\par
2. Build dependencies in a builder stage and run only production artifacts in the final stage.\par
3. Run as a non-root user with a read-only filesystem where possible.\par
4. Set CPU and memory requests and limits after load testing.\par
5. Tag images with a commit SHA, never only latest.\par
6. Scan images and dependencies before pushing to Azure Container Registry.\par
\par
\b Representative backend Dockerfile\b0\par
\f1 FROM node:20-alpine AS build\par
WORKDIR /app\par
COPY package*.json ./\par
RUN npm ci\par
COPY . .\par
RUN npm run build\par
\par
FROM node:20-alpine\par
WORKDIR /app\par
ENV NODE_ENV=production\par
COPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist\par
USER node\par
EXPOSE 3000\par
CMD ["node", "dist/server.js"]\f0\par
\par
\b Representative frontend production Dockerfile\b0\par
\f1 FROM node:20-alpine AS build\par
WORKDIR /app\par
COPY package*.json ./\par
RUN npm ci\par
COPY . .\par
RUN npm run build\par
\par
FROM nginx:alpine\par
COPY --from=build /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html\par
EXPOSE 80\par
CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]\f0\par
\page
\fs26\b 4. NGINX, load balancer, and reverse proxy\b0\fs20\par
Traffic flow:\par
1. Azure Load Balancer receives the public IP and forwards TCP/HTTP traffic to the NGINX Ingress service.\par
2. NGINX terminates TLS when that is the selected policy.\par
3. NGINX applies host/path rules, limits, headers, timeouts, and access logging.\par
4. NGINX forwards requests to internal Kubernetes Services.\par
5. Kubernetes Services distribute traffic to ready pods.\par
6. Backend pods call only internal services or approved private endpoints.\par
\par
Routing policy:\par
- shop.example.com/ -> frontend service\par
- shop.example.com/api/auth -> authentication service\par
- shop.example.com/api/books -> catalog service\par
- shop.example.com/api/search -> search service\par
- shop.example.com/api/cart -> cart service\par
- shop.example.com/api/orders -> order service\par
- shop.example.com/api/payments/webhook -> payment service\par
- admin.example.com/ -> admin service with stricter access policy\par
\par
\b Representative NGINX Kubernetes routing\b0\par
\f1 apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\par
kind: Ingress\par
metadata:\par
  name: bookshop-edge\par
  annotations:\par
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-body-size: 2m\par
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-read-timeout: "30"\par
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-rps: "20"\par
spec:\par
  ingressClassName: nginx\par
  tls:\par
  - hosts: [shop.example.com]\par
    secretName: shop-tls\par
  rules:\par
  - host: shop.example.com\par
    http:\par
      paths:\par
      - path: /api/books\par
        pathType: Prefix\par
        backend:\par
          service:\par
            name: catalog\par
            port:\par
              number: 80\par
      - path: /api/cart\par
        pathType: Prefix\par
        backend:\par
          service:\par
            name: cart\par
            port:\par
              number: 80\par
      - path: /\par
        pathType: Prefix\par
        backend:\par
          service:\par
            name: frontend\par
            port:\par
              number: 80\f0\par
\par
Security decisions:\par
- Put NGINX behind Azure Load Balancer; do not expose every service with a public LoadBalancer service.\par
- Use a WAF at the approved edge if required. Do not duplicate conflicting TLS and WAF policies.\par
- Configure CORS only for genuinely cross-origin clients. Same-host /api routing usually avoids unnecessary CORS.\par
- Set security headers, request limits, upstream timeouts, and access-log redaction.\par
\page
\fs26\b 5. Redis implementation\b0\fs20\par
Use Azure Cache for Redis in production unless there is a strong reason to operate Redis in AKS.\par
\par
Use cases:\par
- Cart cache or short-lived anonymous cart\par
- Session or token revocation data when required\par
- Rate limiting counters\par
- Idempotency keys with a defined retention period\par
- Catalog and search cache\par
\par
Rules:\par
1. Define key prefixes by service, for example cart:user:{id} and idem:payment:{key}.\par
2. Set TTLs for every non-permanent key.\par
3. Never make Redis the source of truth for orders, payments, or stock.\par
4. Define behavior on cache miss and Redis outage.\par
5. Configure TLS, authentication, private endpoint, firewall, and least-privilege access.\par
6. Monitor memory, evictions, hit rate, connections, latency, and failover.\par
7. Test reconnect and fallback behavior.\par
\par
Representative configuration concept:\par
\f1 REDIS_URL=rediss://bookshop-cache.internal:6380\par
REDIS_DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS=900\par
CART_KEY_PREFIX=cart:\par
IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_PREFIX=idem:\f0\par
\par
Redis is shared infrastructure, but ownership of each key namespace remains with the service that creates it.\par
\page
\fs26\b 6. Kafka messaging implementation\b0\fs20\par
Use Azure Event Hubs Kafka compatibility, managed Kafka, or an approved Kafka operator. Choose based on required Kafka semantics, support, and corporate standards. Do not run a single broker in production.\par
\par
Topic examples:\par
- catalog.book.v1\par
- inventory.stock.v1\par
- cart.updated.v1\par
- orders.order-created.v1\par
- payments.payment-status.v1\par
- shipping.shipment-status.v1\par
- notifications.delivery.v1\par
- reporting.business-events.v1\par
\par
Implementation steps:\par
1. Define event ownership, schema, key, partition strategy, retention, and compatibility policy.\par
2. Use the aggregate ID as the message key when per-aggregate ordering matters.\par
3. Give every worker a stable consumer group.\par
4. Add schema validation and backward-compatible evolution.\par
5. Use an outbox table so a committed database change and its event cannot diverge.\par
6. Use retries with a limit, then route to a dead-letter topic.\par
7. Record event ID, correlation ID, producer, schema version, and timestamp.\par
8. Make consumers idempotent because delivery can be repeated.\par
9. Monitor consumer lag, throughput, errors, retries, dead letters, and partition skew.\par
10. Document replay, reprocessing, and poison-message procedures.\par
\par
Representative consumer environment:\par
\f1 KAFKA_BROKERS=bookshop-events.servicebus.windows.net:9093\par
KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL=SASL_SSL\par
KAFKA_CONSUMER_GROUP=notification-service\par
KAFKA_TOPIC=orders.order-created.v1\f0\par
\par
Do not put passwords or connection strings in source control. Use workload identity or Key Vault references.\par
\page
\fs26\b 7. AKS cluster implementation\b0\fs20\par
1. Create a private or appropriately restricted AKS cluster in the approved region.\par
2. Use separate system and user node pools. Use availability zones.\par
3. Enable managed identity, ACR integration, Azure Monitor, network policy, and cluster autoscaler.\par
4. Create namespaces such as bookshop-dev, bookshop-test, and bookshop-prod.\par
5. Install NGINX Ingress, metrics collection, policy controls, and the secrets integration approved by the platform team.\par
6. Create internal ClusterIP Services for all backend APIs.\par
7. Create Deployments with probes, resource settings, disruption budgets, and topology spread.\par
8. Use managed database, Redis, messaging, search, storage, and Key Vault services through private connectivity.\par
\par
\b Representative Deployment and Service\b0\par
\f1 apiVersion: apps/v1\par
kind: Deployment\par
metadata:\par
  name: catalog\par
spec:\par
  replicas: 3\par
  selector:\par
    matchLabels:\par
      app: catalog\par
  template:\par
    metadata:\par
      labels:\par
        app: catalog\par
    spec:\par
      containers:\par
      - name: catalog\par
        image: registry.example.com/bookshop/catalog:COMMIT_SHA\par
        ports:\par
        - containerPort: 3000\par
        resources:\par
          requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi }\par
          limits: { cpu: 500m, memory: 512Mi }\par
        readinessProbe:\par
          httpGet: { path: /ready, port: 3000 }\par
        livenessProbe:\par
          httpGet: { path: /live, port: 3000 }\par
        startupProbe:\par
          httpGet: { path: /startup, port: 3000 }\par
---\par
apiVersion: v1\par
kind: Service\par
metadata:\par
  name: catalog\par
spec:\par
  type: ClusterIP\par
  selector:\par
    app: catalog\par
  ports:\par
  - port: 80\par
    targetPort: 3000\f0\par
\par
A readiness failure removes a pod from traffic. A liveness failure restarts it. A startup probe protects slow-starting services from premature restarts.\par
\page
\fs26\b 8. Autoscaling implementation\b0\fs20\par
\b Horizontal Pod Autoscaler\b0\par
Use HPA for stateless APIs. Start with CPU/memory, then add request rate or latency metrics through the approved metrics adapter.\par
\f1 apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\par
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\par
metadata:\par
  name: catalog\par
spec:\par
  scaleTargetRef:\par
    apiVersion: apps/v1\par
    kind: Deployment\par
    name: catalog\par
  minReplicas: 3\par
  maxReplicas: 20\par
  behavior:\par
    scaleUp: { stabilizationWindowSeconds: 0 }\par
    scaleDown: { stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300 }\par
  metrics:\par
  - type: Resource\par
    resource:\par
      name: cpu\par
      target: { type: Utilization, averageUtilization: 65 }\f0\par
\par
\b KEDA\b0\par
Use KEDA for notification, reporting, search-index, and other workers that scale from Kafka lag or queue depth. Define minimum replicas, maximum replicas, polling interval, cooldown, and failure behavior.\par
\par
\b Cluster Autoscaler\b0\par
Use cluster autoscaler to add nodes when pods cannot be scheduled. Define node-pool minimum and maximum size. Ensure pod requests are realistic, otherwise node scaling signals will be wrong.\par
\par
Scaling test sequence:\par
1. Generate baseline traffic.\par
2. Increase API requests and verify HPA adds pods.\par
3. Increase Kafka backlog and verify KEDA adds consumers.\par
4. Fill node capacity and verify cluster autoscaler adds nodes.\par
5. Reduce load and confirm stabilization avoids flapping.\par
6. Verify scale-down does not interrupt active requests.\par
\page
\fs26\b 9. Data, security, and reliability\b0\fs20\par
\b Durable data\b0\par
Prefer Azure Database for PostgreSQL or Azure SQL. Define schema ownership, migration process, connection pooling, backups, encryption, point-in-time restore, replicas, failover, retention, and restore drills.\par
\par
\b Secrets and identity\b0\par
Use Azure Key Vault and workload identity. Use Kubernetes Secrets only as references or synchronized values under the approved policy. Rotate database, Redis, Kafka, payment, and certificate credentials.\par
\par
\b Network security\b0\par
Use private endpoints for managed services, network policies between namespaces, restricted egress, internal service discovery, and RBAC with least privilege.\par
\par
\b Resilience patterns\b0\par
- Idempotency keys for checkout, payment, reservation, and webhooks.\par
- Timeouts and bounded exponential retry.\par
- Circuit breakers for external providers.\par
- Outbox/inbox processing for event consistency.\par
- Dead-letter topics and operator replay.\par
- Graceful degradation when search, cache, or notification is unavailable.\par
- PodDisruptionBudgets and zone spreading.\par
- No permanent data on container local disk.\par
\par
Failure tests:\par
Pod crash, node loss, zone loss, Redis outage, Kafka consumer outage, database failover, payment timeout, duplicate webhook, provider outage, and partial deployment.\par
\page
\fs26\b 10. CI/CD and operations\b0\fs20\par
Pipeline sequence:\par
1. Checkout source.\par
2. Install exact dependencies from lockfiles.\par
3. Run lint, unit, contract, integration, and security tests.\par
4. Run frontend build and backend builds.\par
5. Run SonarQube analysis and quality gate.\par
6. Build images and generate SBOM.\par
7. Scan images and dependencies.\par
8. Push immutable images to Azure Container Registry.\par
9. Deploy to development and run smoke tests.\par
10. Promote to test/staging with approval.\par
11. Run load and resilience tests.\par
12. Deploy production with rolling, canary, or blue-green strategy.\par
13. Roll back on readiness, error-rate, latency, or business-SLO failure.\par
\par
\b Representative GitHub Actions pipeline\b0\par
\f1 name: bookshop-ci\par
on: [push, pull_request]\par
jobs:\par
  verify:\par
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\par
    steps:\par
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4\par
    - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\par
      with:\par
        node-version: 20\par
        cache: npm\par
    - run: npm ci\par
    - run: npm run lint\par
    - run: npm test --if-present\par
    - run: npm run build\par
    - run: docker build -t bookshop:${{ github.sha }} .\f0\par
\par
Corporate repositories may require approved action versions, internal npm registries, private runners, and an internal SonarQube URL.\par
\par
\b Observability\b0\par
Collect structured logs, traces, metrics, NGINX access/error metrics, API latency/error rate, pod restarts, node pressure, HPA/KEDA state, Redis hit rate and evictions, Kafka lag, database connections, payment outcomes, and checkout success.\par
\par
Operational runbooks must cover deployment rollback, dead-letter replay, Redis failover, Kafka lag, database restore, certificate renewal, secret rotation, and provider outage.\par
\page
\fs26\b 11. Recommended implementation order\b0\fs20\par
1. Baseline the existing frontend build and lint.\par
2. Define service contracts, event schemas, data ownership, and identity decisions.\par
3. Create backend service template and local dependencies.\par
4. Implement authentication and user profile.\par
5. Implement catalog, inventory, cart, and order.\par
6. Integrate the frontend with the APIs; remove reliance on in-memory catalog/cart data.\par
7. Add managed database, Redis, and outbox processing.\par
8. Add payment sandbox, shipping sandbox, notification worker, and Kafka event flows.\par
9. Create AKS, ACR, Key Vault, monitoring, private networking, and NGINX ingress.\par
10. Deploy the first vertical slice with at least two replicas per API.\par
11. Add HPA, KEDA, cluster autoscaler, resource tuning, and load tests.\par
12. Add search, wishlist, reviews, admin, and reporting.\par
13. Add SonarQube, image scans, CI/CD promotion, canary or blue-green release, and rollback.\par
14. Complete security, restore, resilience, cost, and production-readiness reviews.\par
\par
\b Production readiness checklist\b0\par
[ ] All services have an owner, API contract, event contract, health endpoints, tests, image, and runbook.\par
[ ] No secret is present in source, image, frontend bundle, or logs.\par
[ ] Only NGINX/approved edge is public; internal services use ClusterIP.\par
[ ] Durable business data is outside pod local storage.\par
[ ] Redis outage, Kafka replay, database restore, payment retry, and rollback are tested.\par
[ ] HPA, KEDA, and node autoscaling are tested under realistic traffic.\par
[ ] Monitoring and alerts cover technical and business SLOs.\par
[ ] CI quality gates and image scans run before deployment.\par
[ ] Production promotion requires approval and has a rollback path.\par
[ ] Cost, RTO, RPO, security, and compliance targets are signed off.\par
\par
\b Final note\b0\par
Docker packages each service. NGINX reverse-proxies and routes requests. Azure Load Balancer exposes the edge. Kubernetes schedules and replaces pods. HPA/KEDA scale pods. Cluster Autoscaler scales nodes. Redis provides fast shared temporary state. Kafka carries durable asynchronous events. Managed Azure databases and storage retain business state.\par
